In KiCad, set the drill file origin to the bottom left of the PCB. Also select
"use drill/place file origin" during fabrication output creation.

In Kicad, configure the alignment hole to be Through Hole and assign it to the net
of the copper it is placed on. For example, GND. That way, there won't be any
extra paths to provide clearance around them.

In Kocad, set the board edge clearance to zer0. This will prevent extra paths around
the board.

In pcb2gcode, use a zwork = -0.2 and for a 45 degree spiral V cutter, use
mill-diameters = 0.17

Configure the CNC to not reduce stepper currents during idle (M906)

When moving the spindle to the starting position (home), move it to the bottom
left of the PCB to match what KiCad is displaying for the drill file origin.

Give a unique diameter for the alignment hole (for example, 3.01 mm for a 3 mm hole).
 Specify this inside the millproject file. Also specify the hole depth

alignment-hole-diameter = 3.01
alignment-hole-depth = -5.5

Make sure the zero-start flag is set in millproject. This is important to guarantee
that the top and bottom layers match up.

zero-start = 1

First do "back" isolation milling, then do "milldrill" which also includes the alignment holes.
Then turn the board around and do "front" isolation milling. And finally do "drill". This
sequence is important, because each isolation milling is also doing surface mapping, and
for this to work, there must be no holes drilled yet.